.TH PS 1
.SH NAME
ps \- process status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ps
[
.B \-a
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ps
prints one line per process in
.BR /proc :
its process id, its state, and its name, which is the last element of
the path it was started from.
.PP
.B \-a
appends, after the name, the arguments the process was started with,
as recorded in
.BI /proc/ n /args
(see
.IR proc (3)).
.PP
.BI /proc/ n /status
holds the state, one of:
.TP
.B Starting
being created, with no thread yet.
.TP
.B Ready
waiting its turn to run.
.TP
.B Running
running.
.TP
.B Blocked
waiting on a lock, a queue, or a call that parks.
.TP
.B Stopped
parked by a stop request until a
.B start
(see
.IR proc (3)).
.TP
.B Dead
its thread has ended; its record is not yet reclaimed.
.TP
.B Exited
ended cleanly, its status waiting for its parent's
.IR wait .
.TP
.B Faulted
ended by a fault, its status waiting for its parent's
.IR wait .
.PD
.SH FILES
.B /proc/*/status
.br
.B /proc/*/args
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/ps/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR kill (1),
.IR ns (1),
.IR proc (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is empty, or
.B "can't open"
if
.B /proc
could not be opened.
A process that ends between the listing and its
.B status
being read is simply left out.
.SH BUGS
There is no
.BR \-n ,
no user time, no system time and no priority, because a process keeps
none of them yet.
There is no
.IR pstree .
